(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["lymphatics"]="<h1>Lymphatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-lymphatic-vessels-if-we-already-have-veins\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins?</h2>\n<p>Every day, capillaries filter fluid and a little protein into the tissues, and most of it doesn't go straight back into the blood. <strong>Capillaries can't take protein back at all.</strong> Without another route, tissues would steadily swell. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\">→ Microcirculation, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The <strong>lymphatic system</strong> is that route. It collects the excess fluid, proteins and particles from the tissues and returns them to the veins. Once inside a lymph vessel, the fluid is called <strong>lymph</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Lymphatic return route\"><span>Capillary filtration</span><b>→</b><span>Interstitial fluid</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph capillaries</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph vessels and ducts</span><b>→</b><span>Venous blood</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>The lymphatics are a main route, not a back-up.</strong> In most tissues, they return most of the fluid filtered from the capillaries. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/microcirculation?section=6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\">→ Microcirculation, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"2-how-does-lymph-enter-a-lymphatic-capillary\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary?</h2>\n<p><strong>Lymph capillaries start as blind-ended tubes</strong> in the tissues, closed at one end like the finger of a glove. Their wall is one layer of endothelial cells whose edges <strong>overlap like roof tiles</strong>. Fine <strong>anchoring filaments</strong> tie these cells to the surrounding tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>One-way flaps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When tissue fluid builds up, it pushes on the overlapping edges, and the anchoring filaments pull them apart, so the <strong>flaps open inwards</strong> and fluid, proteins and even bacteria flow in.</li>\n<li>When pressure inside the lymph capillary rises, the flaps are <strong>pushed shut</strong>, so nothing leaks back out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is how <strong>proteins</strong> get back into the circulation. They are too big to re-enter blood capillaries, but they pass easily through the lymphatic flaps.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-can-lymph-move-when-there-is-no-central-lymphatic-heart\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart?</h2>\n<p>Lymph flows at very low pressure, with no heart to pump it. Several things keep it moving:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The vessels pump themselves.</strong> Larger lymph vessels have smooth muscle that contracts rhythmically, segment by segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Valves</strong> inside the vessels allow flow only towards the heart.</li>\n<li><strong>The skeletal muscle pump.</strong> Moving muscles squeeze the lymph vessels, just as they squeeze veins. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/venous-return?section=4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work\">→ Venous Return, §4</a>)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Breathing.</strong> Pressure changes in the chest and abdomen draw lymph upwards.</li>\n<li><strong>Pulsing arteries</strong> next to lymph vessels squeeze them with each beat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Like veins, but with one key difference:</strong> veins continue on from blood capillaries in a closed circuit. Lymphatics <strong>start blind, in the tissue</strong>, and only join the blood at the very end.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why do we need lymphatics if capillaries already return some fluid?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillaries can't take back protein that has leaked into the tissues</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Lymphatics carry oxygen to tissues that capillaries miss</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Capillaries only filter fluid at night, so lymph covers the day</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Lymphatics are needed only when blood pressure is high</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:0ecb3afd\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Only the lymphatics can return escaped protein, and in most tissues they also return most of the filtered fluid.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How do lymph capillaries let fluid in but not out?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. They have thick muscular walls that pump fluid in</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Their overlapping endothelial flaps open inwards and shut against backflow</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Proteins inside them pull fluid in by oncotic pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. They are connected directly to the blood capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:ff7cb927\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Fluid pressure opens the overlapping edges inwards. A rise in pressure inside pushes them shut.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Lymph has no heart to pump it. Which of these does NOT help it move?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Contractions of smooth muscle in the lymph vessel walls</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. One-way valves inside the lymph vessels</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Squeezing by nearby skeletal muscles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Pumping by the lymph nodes</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:08e35db0\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Lymph nodes filter lymph for the immune system. They are not pumps.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-where-does-lymph-finally-return-to-blood\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Where does lymph finally return to blood?</h2>\n<p>All lymph drains into two main ducts. Each empties into the veins at the <strong>venous angle</strong>, where the <span data-term=\"jvp\">internal jugular vein</span> joins the subclavian vein:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Duct</th>\n  <th>What it drains</th>\n  <th>Where it empties</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Thoracic duct</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>Everything except the right upper quarter</strong>: both legs, the abdomen, the left side of the chest, left arm, left head and neck</td>\n  <td><strong>Left</strong> venous angle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Right lymphatic duct</strong></td>\n  <td><strong>The right upper quarter only</strong>: the right side of the head and neck, the right arm, the right side of the chest</td>\n  <td><strong>Right</strong> venous angle</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure lymph-duct-figure\" id=\"cvs15-ducts\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Which duct drains where?</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 400 430\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Front view of a body. The patient's right side of the head and neck, right arm and right side of the chest are shaded as drained by the right lymphatic duct. Everything else, including both legs, the abdomen and the whole left side, is shaded as drained by the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct runs up the middle of the body to the left venous angle at the base of the neck; the short right lymphatic duct ends at the right venous angle.\"><defs><clipPath id=\"ly-ruq\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"200\" height=\"190\"/></clipPath><g id=\"ly-trunk\"><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"52\" r=\"30\"/><rect x=\"188\" y=\"80\" width=\"24\" height=\"22\"/><path d=\"M130 102 H270 Q282 104 280 120 L262 262 H138 L120 120 Q118 104 130 102 Z\"/></g></defs><g class=\"ly-td\"><use href=\"#ly-trunk\"/><rect x=\"88\" y=\"108\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\"/><rect x=\"284\" y=\"108\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\"/><rect x=\"142\" y=\"262\" width=\"50\" height=\"150\" rx=\"16\"/><rect x=\"208\" y=\"262\" width=\"50\" height=\"150\" rx=\"16\"/></g><g class=\"ly-rd\"><use href=\"#ly-trunk\" clip-path=\"url(#ly-ruq)\"/><rect x=\"88\" y=\"108\" width=\"28\" height=\"150\" rx=\"14\"/></g><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"190\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"190\" class=\"ly-dia\"/><path d=\"M200 232 C204 190 196 150 202 118 Q206 104 222 100\" class=\"ly-duct\"/><path d=\"M190 116 Q184 106 176 100\" class=\"ly-duct\"/><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" class=\"ly-angle\"/><circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"100\" r=\"5\" class=\"ly-angle\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"24\" class=\"ly-lab ly-lab-rd\">Right lymphatic</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"42\" class=\"ly-lab ly-lab-rd\">duct:</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"62\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">right upper quarter</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"330\" class=\"ly-lab ly-lab-td\">Thoracic duct:</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"346\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">everything else</text><text x=\"232\" y=\"96\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">left venous angle</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"96\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\">right venous angle →</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"182\" class=\"ly-lab-xs\">diaphragm level</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"425\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\" text-anchor=\"middle\">patient's right</text><text x=\"298\" y=\"425\" class=\"ly-lab-sm\" text-anchor=\"middle\">patient's left</text></svg><p>Front view (the patient's right is on your left). The thoracic duct starts in the abdomen and runs up the middle of the body to the left venous angle. The short right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper quarter, into the right venous angle.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Memory hook:</strong> the thoracic duct takes <strong>three-quarters</strong> of the body; the right lymphatic duct takes the <strong>right upper quarter</strong>.</p>\n<h3>What do lymph nodes do?</h3>\n<p>On its way, lymph passes through <strong>lymph nodes</strong>: bean-shaped filters packed with immune cells. They check the lymph for bacteria, viruses and cancer cells. That is why nodes swell during an infection, and why cancer often spreads first to the nearest nodes. <strong>Nodes filter; they don't pump.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-is-the-intestinal-lymphatic-system-important-in-fat-absorption\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption?</h2>\n<p>Each finger-like villus of the small intestine contains a lymph capillary called a <strong>lacteal</strong> (<em>lac</em> = milk, after the milky look of lymph after a fatty meal).</p>\n<p><strong>How fat gets into the blood:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Intestinal lipid absorption through lymphatics\"><span>Long-chain dietary lipids</span><b>→</b><span>Enterocyte chylomicrons</span><b>→</b><span>Villus lacteal</span><b>→</b><span>Mesenteric lymph</span><b>→</b><span>Thoracic duct → Blood</span></div>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Gut cells absorb the digested fats and repackage them into large fat droplets wrapped in protein: <strong>chylomicrons</strong>.</li>\n<li>Chylomicrons are <strong>too big</strong> to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the leaky <strong>lacteals</strong> instead.</li>\n<li>They travel up the lymphatics and the thoracic duct, and enter the blood at the left <span data-term=\"venous-angle\">venous angle</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This milky, fat-rich lymph from the gut is called <strong>chyle</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Not all fat goes this way.</strong> Short- and medium-chain fatty acids are small enough to go straight into the blood capillaries, and on to the liver in the <span data-term=\"portal-vein\">portal vein</span>. It is the <strong>long-chain fats</strong> (and fat-soluble vitamins) that need the lymph.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–5\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–5 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Lymph from the left leg reaches the blood through which duct?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The right lymphatic duct, at the right venous angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The thoracic duct, at the left venous angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The thoracic duct, at the right venous angle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The portal vein, through the liver</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:82aa38fd\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The thoracic duct drains everything except the right upper quarter, and empties at the left venous angle.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why do long-chain dietary fats enter lacteals rather than blood capillaries?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Blood capillaries in the gut have no openings</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. They are packaged into chylomicrons, too big for blood capillaries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Lacteals actively pump fat out of the gut cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Fat would raise blood pressure if it entered the blood directly</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:d2cf429f\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Chylomicrons are large particles. They pass through the leaky lacteal wall but not into blood capillaries.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why do lymph nodes in the neck swell during a throat infection?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Lymph backs up behind the nodes because they stop pumping</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Immune cells in the nodes multiply as they respond to the infection</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Fat from the throat collects in the nodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The nodes fill with blood from inflamed capillaries</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:e3280ddd\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Nodes are immune filters. When they trap microbes, their immune cells multiply and the nodes enlarge.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-lymphatic-drainage-protect-tissue-fluid-balance\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance?</h2>\n<p>The lymphatics keep the tissues from swelling by matching what the capillaries filter. When filtration rises, lymph flow can rise several-fold to keep up, which is a safety margin. <strong>Oedema appears only when filtration outstrips this, or when the lymphatics themselves fail.</strong> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=1-what-exactly-is-oedema\">→ Oedema, §1</a>)</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Problem</th>\n  <th>What fails</th>\n  <th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Too much filtration</strong></td>\n  <td>The lymphatics can't keep up</td>\n  <td>Venous congestion, heart failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Too little drainage</strong></td>\n  <td>Normal filtration, but it can't be removed</td>\n  <td>Filariasis, surgery that removes lymph nodes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-lymphoedema\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema?</h2>\n<p>When lymphatics are blocked, protein-rich fluid builds up. The protein holds the water in the tissue, and over time causes inflammation and scarring. The result is <strong>lymphoedema</strong>: swelling that is soft at first and becomes <strong>firm and non-pitting</strong> as it lasts. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/edema?section=8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different\">→ Oedema, §8</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The lecture's examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Filariasis:</strong> parasitic worms (spread by mosquitoes) live in and damage the lymph vessels, and can cause the gross swelling of <strong>elephantiasis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Radical mastectomy:</strong> removing the armpit lymph nodes in breast cancer surgery can leave the arm swollen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cancer blocking lymph nodes, and lymphatics that never formed properly (congenital), can do the same.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-what-happens-if-lymphatic-function-is-lost\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> What happens if lymphatic function is lost?</h2>\n<p>Three jobs fail:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fluid balance:</strong> fluid and protein stay in the tissues, causing <span data-term=\"lymphoedema\">lymphoedema</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>Immune defence:</strong> lymph no longer reaches the nodes, so infections are detected and fought less well, and swollen limbs get infected easily.</li>\n<li><strong>Fat absorption:</strong> if the gut lymphatics fail, long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins can't be absorbed properly.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 6–8\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 6–8 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–8</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Filtration into a tissue doubles, but the tissue doesn't swell. Why?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Capillaries reabsorb all the extra fluid at the venous end</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Lymph flow rises to carry away the extra fluid</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The extra fluid is absorbed by the tissue cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Filtration can't double without the tissue swelling</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:a725565d\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Lymph flow can increase several-fold, a safety margin against oedema.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A man from an area where filariasis is common has one hugely swollen, thickened leg. What is the mechanism?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Worms in the lymph vessels block drainage, so protein-rich fluid builds up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Worms in the veins block blood returning from the leg</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The infection lowers his plasma albumin</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The worms release histamine that makes capillaries leaky</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:2149b0c6\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Filarial worms damage the lymphatics. Protein-rich fluid accumulates, and chronic inflammation causes the thick, firm swelling of elephantiasis.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which problem would loss of the gut lymphatics specifically cause?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Inability to absorb glucose from the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Inability to absorb long-chain fats and fat-soluble vitamins</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Inability to absorb water from the gut</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Inability to absorb amino acids from the gut</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"lymphatics:8bccd8b3\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Long-chain fats travel as chylomicrons through the lacteals. Glucose, amino acids and water go straight into the blood.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">9.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lymphatics return</strong> excess tissue fluid and <strong>all escaped protein</strong> to the blood. They are a main route, not a back-up.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph capillaries</strong> are blind-ended, with overlapping flaps that open inwards and shut against backflow.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph moves</strong> by vessel-wall contractions, valves, the muscle pump, breathing and pulsing arteries. There is no heart, and nodes don't pump.</li>\n<li><strong>Thoracic duct</strong> drains three-quarters of the body into the <strong>left</strong> venous angle. <strong>Right lymphatic duct</strong> drains the right upper quarter into the <strong>right</strong> venous angle.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph nodes</strong> filter lymph for the immune system.</li>\n<li><strong>Lacteals</strong> carry long-chain fats as <strong>chylomicrons</strong>. Milky gut lymph is <strong>chyle</strong>. Short- and medium-chain fats go straight to the blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph flow can rise several-fold</strong> to prevent oedema. Swelling appears when filtration outstrips it or drainage fails.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphoedema</strong> (filariasis, node removal, cancer): protein-rich, becoming firm and non-pitting.</li>\n<li><strong>Lost lymphatics</strong> → swelling (fluid and protein are left in the tissues), weaker immune defence (lymph no longer reaches the nodes' immune cells) and poor fat absorption (lacteals can't carry chylomicrons).</li>\n</ol>\n";
